\section{Extracting a Helper With Explicit Free Variables}

A match can select an expression to extract into a named helper.
The interface of the helper must account for the bindings that
the original expression used. Consider a lexically scoped,
strict Lisp subset with immutable variables, left-to-right calls,
and no observations of stack depth or allocation identity.
Choose an expression $e$ and a legal insertion scope for a fresh
helper with no captures. Resolve free references before moving
anything. The output is a helper definition, a replacement call
at the original location, and a map between original binding
identities and introduced formals. Include references in nested
lambdas when computing the free variables of the selection.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input resolved expression $e$, helper scope $H$
  \Output helper and replacement, or refusal
  \State $B\gets\Call{FreeBindings}{e}$
  \Comment{Set of identities, not spellings}
  \State order $B$ by first reference in $e$
  \State $f\gets\Call{FreshHelper}{H}$
  \State $\rho\gets$ empty binding map
  \ForAll{$b$ in $B$}
    \If{$b$ is not an immutable value binding}
      \State \Return refusal
    \EndIf
    \State $\rho[b]\gets\Call{FreshFormal}{}$
  \EndFor
  \State $e'\gets\Call{RenameFree}{e,\rho}$
  \Comment{Do not rewrite local binders}
  \State $d\gets\Call{Define}{f,\rho[B],e'}$
  \State $A\gets\Call{References}{B}$
  \State $c\gets\Call{Apply}{f,A}$
  \State \Return $(d,c,\rho)$
\end{sudocode}

In a scope with $x=4$, select the body
\verb|(add x ((lambda (x) (mul x 2)) 3))|.
Treat \texttt{add} and \texttt{mul} as immutable procedure
bindings. The free set in first-use order is \texttt{add},
outer \texttt{x}, and \texttt{mul}; the inner \texttt{x}
is bound within the expression. The helper receives three
arguments. With formals named \texttt{a}, \texttt{v}, and
\texttt{m}, its body is
\verb|(a v ((lambda (x) (m x 2)) 3))|.
Calling it with the three original bindings computes $4+6=10$.
A spelling-based rename of both occurrences of \texttt{x}
could instead compute $4+8=12$.

The invariant is that each external reference reads the same value
through its formal, and each local reference still targets the
original local binder within the copied tree. Parameter reads can
move ahead of the body only because they are total, effect-free
reads of already initialized immutable bindings. Mutable, special,
or uninitialized bindings require a different rule. If the helper
has no parameters, still emit a call at the original evaluation
point; hoisting the body itself could run its effects prematurely.
